WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.350
All right this one's definitely a little
strange so buckle up we're going to talk

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:16.890
about weird kids videos and gaming the
algorithm.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:21.240
if you don't have kids you quite understandably probably don't watch many YouTube videos intended for

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:26.670
kids and when I say kids really I mean
babies. We're going to be talking about

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:30.599
the YouTube equivalent of Teletubbies
and Blue's Clues, videos aimed at a

00:00:30.599 --> 00:00:35.070
preschool audience. I bring that up first
because as we go through all of this

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:38.640
it's helpful to keep in mind that the
target audience isn't really making

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.710
coherent viewing decisions. Preschool
audiences are largely passive viewers,

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:47.340
watching whatever is put in front of
them, rather than seeking media out.

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:51.449
there's a lot of content on YouTube for
preschoolers that is more or less what

00:00:51.449 --> 00:00:55.770
you would expect. pretty much any popular
television show like Sesame Street and

00:00:55.770 --> 00:01:00.420
Peppa Pig also has a substantial
presence on YouTube. In fact a compelling

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.530
argument could be made that Peppa Pig is
more popular online than through

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:08.040
broadcast television.
But as with any popular YouTube genre

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.909
there is an attendant flood of
low-quality imitators and bootleggers.

00:01:11.909 --> 00:01:15.720
Much of it is exactly what you would
expect: people uploading stuff they

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.250
couldn't find online or just re
uploading stuff to skim ad revenue. Then

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:25.320
there's the YouTube-specific genres, such
as surprise egg videos toy unboxings and

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:28.979
Nursery Rhymes; things that are more or
less what you'd expect from the phrase

00:01:28.979 --> 00:01:35.850
YouTube but for kids. You know, YouTube without all the cussing.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:40.189
but there's also a lot that's decidedly off

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:45.820
just something about it

00:01:54.610 --> 00:02:00.320
yeah. So you may already be familiar with
this particular sub-genre of children's

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:06.229
media. If you delve into it now in 2017
what you'll find is a hallucinatory mix

00:02:06.229 --> 00:02:10.280
of popular characters being run through
a gristle mill of family finger songs,

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:14.959
wheels on the bus, monkeys on the bed,
wrong heads, Johnny Johnny yes papa,

00:02:14.959 --> 00:02:19.400
color and shape matching, and skits
revolving around pregnancy, poop, and

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:24.830
adults randomly kicking the crap out of
kids. A popular recent article by James

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:28.910
Bridle titled "something is wrong on the
Internet" takes a fairly broad dive into

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:33.250
this subject, focusing on the generally
unsettling nature of many of the videos.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:38.930
youtuber h3h3 has done a popular video
on people intentionally hijacking the

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:42.410
genre with adult content.
Fredrik Knudesen of Down the Rabbit Hole

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:47.180
has done a video on the history of the
family finger song in specific, and let's

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:50.959
play channel Retsupuray has done a
number of commentaries for animated

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:55.519
videos with titles like "Mickey and
Minnie Mouse Baby Missing Abducted

00:02:55.519 --> 00:03:00.250
Lethal Injection Mickey Mouse Clubhouse
Cartoons Full." The moral of the story

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:07.730
don't murder children I guess? I mean
that's alright. Now the actual origins

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:12.440
here are muddled, starting in India in
the late 90s with low-grade animated

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:17.870
rhyming video sold on cheap video CDs.
The jump to YouTube starts around 2010

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:21.920
with the animation companies basically
just uploading their existing catalogue,

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:26.480
but it doesn't really break into the
attention of the Anglosphere until 2013

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:31.190
2014 with the proliferation of family
finger song videos, and channels

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:35.870
dedicated to posting minute variants of a
basic template. With each year as new

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:40.190
styles of children's videos gain
popularity their format is simply folded

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:45.049
into the mix. The wordless superhero
skits genre in particular is a fairly

00:03:45.049 --> 00:03:50.060
new addition, exploding almost out of
nowhere in mid-2016 with adults dressed

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:53.930
in popular character costumes (almost
universally including spider-man and

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:59.360
Elsa) acting out low-quality absurdist
skits. The rapid proliferation is largely

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:04.070
comprehensible: the material barriers to
entry are low requiring only a handful

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:06.290
of Halloween costumes some friends and
a

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:10.159
lack of dignity; and the standards for
quality among four-year-olds are even

00:04:10.159 --> 00:04:14.299
lower than average for YouTube. As with
the family finger song and a dozen other

00:04:14.299 --> 00:04:19.070
subgenres this too has metastasized
rapidly into a procedurally generated

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:23.150
animation hellscape. A lot of the
disturbing elements can be attributed to

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:26.990
this culturally disconnected history.
These are for the most part being

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:31.280
cranked out by South Asian animation
companies that, for any of a variety of

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:36.289
reasons, don't share the American ideoform of what acceptable children's

00:04:36.289 --> 00:04:40.639
entertainment looks like, or maybe they
simply don't care. There's an odd

00:04:40.639 --> 00:04:44.210
interplay of memetics, here where someone will make something with the express

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:47.570
purpose of trolling people;
intentionally disturbing children's

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:52.310
animation for example; that will gain
popularity and subsequently be absorbed

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:56.900
into the meme-body of these channels
churning out content programmatically.

00:04:56.900 --> 00:05:00.830
Bridal's article in particular spends a
good deal of time highlighting the

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:04.880
algorithmic structure of these videos:
hundreds of channels uploading hundreds

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:09.860
of videos all operating off the same
recombinantory logic of AI procedural

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:14.840
generation, Bridal making a direct
comparison to automated on-demand Amazon

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:19.220
stores that place stock images on a
variety of products from coffee mugs to

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:23.419
cell phone covers. These children's
videos form a sort of inverse Turing

00:05:23.419 --> 00:05:27.139
test as whether they are made by
individuals who just don't care beyond

00:05:27.139 --> 00:05:31.520
min-maxing their search engine
optimization, or are made by programs

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:36.020
smashing elements together from a word
cloud, it is almost impossible to tell

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:39.680
the difference.
Even if made by humans they function as

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:43.780
though they were assembled by machines.

00:05:44.979 --> 00:05:49.520
For example this video titled
superheroes pregnant soccer balls fidget

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:53.990
spinner Spider Man Joker Hulk cartoon
funny kids video pranks is a series

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:58.639
of semi-coherent, however surreal,
segments including one where joker sells

00:05:58.639 --> 00:06:02.810
Hulk and spider-man magical fidget
spinners; pregnant Elsa and spider-man at

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:11.169
the supermarket; and a bunch of
superheroes pouring M&amp;Ms into a blender?

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:16.630
Now that last one is actually repeated
twice in the same video, and the other

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:21.010
two segments can also be found in other
videos. Joker's magic fidget spinners can

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:25.450
also be found in this video titled
spider-man Hulk pregnant frozen Elsa

00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:31.600
colors gummy fidget spinner Joker prank
venom fun superheroes, and pregnant Elsa

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:35.800
and spider-man at the supermarket is the
lead segment in the video frozen elsa

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:39.550
pregnant colors superhero spider-man
Hulk vs venom Joker fidget spinners

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:48.520
colors for kids. The point is that the
videos themselves are procedurally

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:52.870
generated from a library of component
videos which are themselves generated

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:57.160
from stock assets, motion capture
libraries, and the handful of subjects

00:06:57.160 --> 00:07:01.750
that four-year-olds find fascinating. The
end result is as Mike Rugnetta put it

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:07.300
metadata slurry. The animated videos are
hallucinatory recreations of the already

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:12.070
surreal originals down to unsettling
levels of specificity. Buried alive

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:16.090
outdoor playground finger family song
nursery rhymes animation education

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:21.070
learning video is clearly a Markov
derivative of the popular video buried

00:07:21.070 --> 00:07:25.270
alive spider-man vs. frozen elsa baby
pink spidergirl joker family fun

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:29.410
superhero movie. One of our previous
examples spider-man and pregnant Elsa

00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:34.330
the supermarket is an eerily specific
recreation of pregnant frozen elsa

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:40.110
giving birth and joker attack spider-man
Hulk superheroes movie compilation.

00:07:51.129 --> 00:07:56.119
And yeah it's wild that the stuff made
by humans is still just word salad but

00:07:56.119 --> 00:08:00.860
you need to keep in mind that the target
audience can't read so the title is

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:06.649
irrelevant beyond its function as search
engine optimization. Now there's already

00:08:06.649 --> 00:08:10.039
something troublesome about the
fundamental structure of YouTube Kids.

00:08:10.039 --> 00:08:14.119
Young children particularly preschoolers
are prone to watching the same thing

00:08:14.119 --> 00:08:20.179
over and over and over. A kid may watch
any given video dozens or even hundreds

00:08:20.179 --> 00:08:24.619
of times. As a result many of these
videos have absurdly high view counts

00:08:24.619 --> 00:08:29.119
with what would in other corners of
YouTube be suspiciously low number of

00:08:29.119 --> 00:08:33.469
corresponding likes and comments. This,
again, makes a bit more sense when you

00:08:33.469 --> 00:08:38.689
consider that most four-year-olds can at
best only read a few words and can maybe

00:08:38.689 --> 00:08:43.099
write their own name. Now that
troublesome structure built on a

00:08:43.099 --> 00:08:46.819
foundation of chain-viewing
four-year-olds gets all the worse when

00:08:46.819 --> 00:08:50.750
you consider that it's a perfect
environment for mechanized exploitation.

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:54.740
The standards for the content are
already so low that it is difficult to

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:58.399
distinguish what is the product of a
machine throwing elements together semi

00:08:58.399 --> 00:09:03.620
randomly and what is the product of
adults who just don't care. The behaviour

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:08.540
patterns of kids chain watching videos
while not otherwise engaging is also

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:13.100
easily replicated by bots, and while
there's no hard proof of any of these

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:18.019
channels botting, it would be almost
effortless to hide if they were.

00:09:18.019 --> 00:09:19.939
And here's where it really goes off the
rails

00:09:19.939 --> 00:09:24.410
Ao as I've already mentioned these
procedural animation videos crop up

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:28.370
across multiple channels. The specific
pregnant elsa segment we've been using

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:32.750
as an example shows up in videos on
channels cartoon animation rhymes, rhymes

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:37.399
lyrics, cartoon rhymes, animals for kids,
rhymes for kids, and who knows how many

00:09:37.399 --> 00:09:42.050
others. In case you're wondering if this
might just be a case of simple theft, all

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:47.829
of these channels belong to the same
multi channel network named 3h uox 580

00:09:47.829 --> 00:09:56.300
USR n6 h wh o kz jsq. It's case sensitive
by the way. I guess i'm saying that the

00:09:56.300 --> 00:10:02.120
odds of them all ending up on that same
MCN are low, you know? There are 58

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:05.540
channels attached to that MCN, almost all of them posting

00:10:05.540 --> 00:10:10.549
nearly identical content based off the
same source library. Everything about it

00:10:10.549 --> 00:10:15.589
screams minimal human interaction. The
strategy here is to cast a very wide net,

00:10:15.589 --> 00:10:21.199
with the illusion of diversity. A single
channel uploading dozens of videos per

00:10:21.199 --> 00:10:26.329
day will quickly trip YouTube spam
alarms, but a network of dozens of

00:10:26.329 --> 00:10:31.910
channels uploading one video each per
day won't. YouTube's search algorithms

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:36.470
and recommended videos try to give you
content that's just like whatever you

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:40.699
just watched, but they're also designed
to force a bit of variety to prevent a

00:10:40.699 --> 00:10:44.839
single channel from totally dominating a
given genre. The distributed spam

00:10:44.839 --> 00:10:49.639
technique works around this, meaning the
algorithm can deliver "diverse" results

00:10:49.639 --> 00:10:53.689
from many different channels while
actually delivering results that all

00:10:53.689 --> 00:10:58.129
point back to the same core entity. But
that's not actually even the end of the

00:10:58.129 --> 00:11:01.669
story. You might notice that I've
stripped the audio entirely from all the

00:11:01.669 --> 00:11:05.989
examples that I've used here and there's
a good reason for that. So here's an

00:11:05.989 --> 00:11:10.069
interesting experiment that I've already
done for you. Re-upload a bunch of these

00:11:10.069 --> 00:11:15.199
videos and watch the copyright claims
come rolling in. I uploaded five videos

00:11:15.199 --> 00:11:20.119
to a burner channel and within half an
hour had 32 copyright claims, all of

00:11:20.119 --> 00:11:25.249
which had the attached policy of a
global block. One video in particular got

00:11:25.249 --> 00:11:29.720
hit with 13 separate claims. That video
was actually one that you might be

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:33.589
familiar with if you read James Bridal's
article, as he included it as an example,

00:11:33.589 --> 00:11:37.069
and that's buried alive outdoor
playground finger family song nursery

00:11:37.069 --> 00:11:41.480
rhymes animation education learning
video. Now here's the breadcrumb trail:

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:45.980
that video is taken from the channel
animals for kids, one of the channels

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:54.649
that's part of the multi channel network
3 Huo X 580 us RN 6 HW h o kz jsq, but

00:11:54.649 --> 00:11:58.759
all of the copyright claims point to the
multi-channel networks mango Kids,

00:11:58.759 --> 00:12:03.259
wacked-out affiliate, and Mobiwalkers-2, with the mango kids being the

00:12:03.259 --> 00:12:07.730
overwhelming source of copyright claims.
The important implication here is the

00:12:07.730 --> 00:12:12.860
disconnect. All of these videos contain
content that is rapidly claimed by three

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:17.030
separate networks, each with a standard
worldwide block policy.

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:20.720
But the content is demonstrably
available on channels under other

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:24.650
networks than the claiming network,
meaning that the automated Content ID

00:12:24.650 --> 00:12:29.930
claims are either being released or
modified. It's not hard proof but it is

00:12:29.930 --> 00:12:33.500
compelling and I feel it's fair to say
there is a good possibility that these

00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:38.780
networks are in fact all the same root
entity. mango Kids, Mobiwalkers,

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:43.280
wacked-out, and three Huo X are all the
same people,

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:46.670
meaning that whichever one is in fact
the originating organization is

00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:51.530
operating at least 400 separate YouTube
channels proliferating a spam of

00:12:51.530 --> 00:12:55.040
children's content, programmatically
dominating search results and

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:58.940
recommended videos. Now if you think that YouTube is unaware of this I would point

00:12:58.940 --> 00:13:02.480
out that the process of getting a
multi-channel network with content

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:07.450
management privileges through YouTube is
not an automated process. There is no

00:13:07.450 --> 00:13:12.320
application form, they are all handled
one at a time by YouTube's content

00:13:12.320 --> 00:13:17.150
management teams. These networks are
comprised entirely of channels that

00:13:17.150 --> 00:13:23.480
exist purely to manipulate YouTube's
algorithms. So why doesn't YouTube care?

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:28.340
Well because as long as advertisers are
unaware of how many of their impressions

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:32.510
are coming from four-year-olds sitting
with an iPad on autoplay then the trade

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:36.950
value of advertising on YouTube will
remain higher than the actual value and

00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:41.690
YouTube will continue making money. The
big reason why YouTube takes measures to

00:13:41.690 --> 00:13:45.800
crack down on egregious examples of bots
watching videos is because advertisers

00:13:45.800 --> 00:13:52.190
don't like the fact that they're paying
for ads shown to no one. Now that said, if

00:13:52.190 --> 00:13:57.350
we're honest and if we use the 1980s as
an indicator there's an awful lot of

00:13:57.350 --> 00:14:02.390
advertisers who are ultimately ok with
their ads being bombarded on to toddlers

00:14:02.390 --> 00:14:07.730
and plenty of creators willing to churn
out low effort content to facilitate that.

00:14:07.730 --> 00:14:22.899
Man, that's a downer ending.

